.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
converts a markup language like HTML or XML into a
.Sy Hiccup
data structure,
which it writes out as Janet source.
With
.Fl "-reverse" ,
the conversion runs the other way and a Hiccup data structure
is converted into markup.
.Pp
Input is read from the file at
.Ar input ,
or from stdin if
.Ar input
is not given.
Output is written to stdout unless
.Fl "-output"
names a file.
.Pp
Output is written on a single line unless
.Fl "-pretty"
is given.
The options that shape the layout,
.Fl "-indent"
and
.Fl "-width" ,
ask for it in the same way,
so either can be given on its own.
More information is set out in
.Sx "Pretty Printing"
below.
.
.Ss Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Fl f
.Ar format No \&|
.Fl "-format"
.Ar format
.Xc
The
.Ar format
of the markup,
either
.Ql "html"
or
.Ql "xml" .
The default is
.Ql "html" .
In HTML,
the elements that cannot have children are fixed by the language
and are written without a closing tag.
In XML,
nothing is void and an element with no children closes itself.
.It Xo
.Fl i
.Ar number No \&|
.Fl "-indent"
.Ar number
.Xc
The
.Ar number
of spaces added for each level of nesting.
The default is 2.
Giving this option implies
.Fl "-pretty" .
This applies whichever way the conversion runs.
.It Xo
.Fl o
.Ar path No \&|
.Fl "-output"
.Ar path
.Xc
Writes the output to the file at
.Ar path .
By default,
the output is written to stdout.
.It Xo
.Fl p No \&|
.Fl "-pretty"
.Xc
Pretty prints the output over multiple lines.
By default,
the output is written on a single line.
.It Xo
.Fl r No \&|
.Fl "-reverse"
.Xc
Reverses the polarity and converts from Hiccup to markup.
The input is read as the Janet source of a Hiccup data structure
rather than as markup.
See
.Sx "CAVEATS"
below.
.It Xo
.Fl w
.Ar number No \&|
.Fl "-width"
.Ar number
.Xc
The
.Ar number
of columns an element is fitted within before it is broken open.
The default is 80.
Giving this option implies
.Fl "-pretty" .
Only Hiccup output is fitted to a width,
so this option is ignored when
.Fl "-reverse"
is given.
.El
.
.Ss Hiccup Syntax\c
.Sy Hiccup
represents an element as an array whose first value is a keyword
naming the element.
An optional table of attributes may follow,
and everything after that is a child of the element.
So the markup:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<h1 class=\(dqtitle\(dq>Hello <em>world</em></h1>
.Ed
.Pp
is represented as:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
@\(lB:h1 @{:class \(dqtitle\(dq} \(dqHello \(dq @\(lB:em \(dqworld\(dq\(rB\(rB
.Ed
.
.Ss Pretty Printing
When markup is converted to Hiccup,
an element is kept on one line where it fits within
.Fl "-width"
columns and none of its children starts a block of its own.
Otherwise its children are placed beneath it,
each indented by a further
.Fl "-indent"
spaces.
The shape of the source therefore follows the shape of the markup
it stands for.
.Pp
When Hiccup is converted to markup,
a newline is only introduced between two nodes where it cannot
change how the markup is interpreted.
The contents of elements like
.Ql "<p>"
and
.Ql "<pre>"
are left alone,
so the pretty printed markup renders the same as the markup
written on a single line.
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
To convert a file of HTML to a Hiccup data structure:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lg page\&.html
.Ed
.Pp
To read from stdin and write the result to a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl -s https://example\&.org | lg -o page\&.janet
.Ed
.Pp
To pretty print the result,
fitting elements within 40 columns and indenting each level
of nesting by 4 spaces:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lg -w 40 -i 4 page\&.html
.Ed
.Pp
To convert a Hiccup data structure back to HTML:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lg -r page\&.janet
.Ed
.Pp
To convert a file of XML:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lg -f xml doc\&.xml
.Ed
.
